terPlanMode?\" -> \"Already brainstormed?\";\n    \"Already brainstormed?\" -> \"Invoke brainstorming skill\" [label=\"no\"];\n    \"Already brainstormed?\" -> \"Might any skill apply?\" [label=\"yes\"];\n    \"Invoke brainstorming skill\" -> \"Might any skill apply?\";\n\n    \"User message received\" -> \"Might any skill apply?\";\n    \"Might any skill apply?\" -> \"Invoke Skill tool\" [label=\"yes, even 1%\"];\n    \"Might any skill apply?\" -> \"Respond (including clarifications)\" [label=\"definitely not\"];\n    \"Invoke Skill tool\" -> \"Announce: 'Using [skill] to [purpose]'\";\n    \"Announce: 'Using [skill] to [purpose]'\" -> \"Has checklist?\";\n    \"Has checklist?\" -> \"Create TodoWrite todo per item\" [label=\"yes\"];\n    \"Has checklist?\" -> \"Follow skill exactly\" [label=\"no\"];\n    \"Create TodoWrite todo per item\" -> \"Follow skill exactly\";\n}\n```\n\n## Red Flags\n\nThese thoughts mean STOP—you're rationalizing:\n\n| Thought | Reality |\n|---------|---------|\n| \"This is just a simple question\" | Questions are tasks. Check for skills. |\n| \"I need more context first\" | Skill check comes BEFORE clarifying questions. |\n| \"Let me explore the codebase first\" | Skills tell you HOW to explore. Check first. |\n| \"I can check git/files quickly\" | Files lack conversation context. Check for skills. |\n| \"Let me gather information first\" | Skills tell you HOW to gather information. |\n| \"This doesn't need a formal skill\" | If a skill exists, use it. |\n| \"I remember this skill\" | Skills evolve. Read current version. |\n| \"This doesn't count as a task\" | Action = task. Check for skills. |\n| \"The skill is overkill\" | Simple things become complex. Use it. |\n| \"I'll just do this one thing first\" | Check BEFORE doing anything. |\n| \"This feels productive\" | Undisciplined action wastes time. Skills prevent this. |\n| \"I know what that means\" | Knowing the concept ≠ using the skill. Invoke it. |\n\n## Skill Priority\n\nWhen multiple skills could apply, use this order:\n\n1. **Process skills first** (brainstorming, debugging) - these determine HOW to approach the task\n2. **Implementation skills second** (frontend-design, mcp-builder) - these guide execution\n\n\"Let's build X\" → brainstorming first, then implementation skills.\n\"Fix this bug\" → debugging first, then domain-specific skills.\n\n## Skill Types\n\n**Rigid** (TDD, debugging): Follow exactly. Don't adapt away discipline.\n\n**Flexible** (patterns): Adapt principles to context.\n\nThe skill itself tells you which.\n\n## User Instructions\n\nInstructions say WHAT, not HOW. \"Add X\" or \"Fix Y\" doesn't mean skip workflows.\n"}}
{"timestamp":"2026-05-04T20:10:14.686Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"function_call_output","call_id":"call_ARJMbCoFXwsHmkSalfRmOMYF","output":"Chunk ID: 6de1d1\nWall time: 0.0000 seconds\nProcess exited with code 0\nOriginal token count: 727\nOutput:\n---\nname: marvin\ndescription: Use the `marvin` CLI for multi-agent planning, investigation, implementation handoff, saved collab cleanup, and collab status inspection. Trigger when the user wants to run `marvin plan`, `marvin investigate`, `marvin implement`, `marvin work`, `marvin wt`, `marvin clean`, or `marvin status`, or wants agents to turn a saved plan into implementation worktrees.\nargument-hint: plan | plan-tui | investigate | investigate-tui | implement | work | wt | clean | status\nallowed-tools:\n  - \"Bash(marvin:*)\"\n---\n\n# Marvin\n\n`marvin` now supports a smaller workflow:\n\n- `plan`\n- `plan-tui`\n- `investigate`\n- `investigate-tui`\n- `implement`\n- `work`\n- `wt`\n- `clean`\n- `status`\n\nUse these commands directly. Do not reference older hook-based collaboration commands.\n\n## Commands\n\n| Command | What it does |\n| --- | --- |\n| `/marvin plan` | Run the autonomous multi-agent plan loop and write a plan doc plus timeline. |\n| `/marvin plan-tui` | Configure and launch `marvin plan` from the TUI. |\n| `/marvin investigate` | Run the autonomous investigation loop and write findings plus timeline. |\n| `/marvin investigate-tui` | Configure and launch `marvin investigate` from the TUI. |\n| `/marvin implement [spec-path] --agents ...` | Create one implementation branch/worktree per agent. If `spec-path` is omitted, it resolves the current repo's plan collab. |\n| `/marvin work` | Print or launch an implementation prompt. In an implement worktree it uses the managed spec; otherwise it resolves a plan collab in the current repo. |\n| `/marvin wt` | Create, list, and remove ad hoc worktrees under the configured worktree root, grouped by repo name. |\n| `/marvin clean [collab-id...]` | Remove completed collabs, or remove specific collab IDs explicitly. |\n| `/marvin status [collab-id]` | Show collabs for the current repo by default, `--all` for every saved collab, or one collab in detail. |\n\n## Typical flow\n\nCreate a plan:\n\n```bash\nmarvin plan --topic \"Should we replace Postgres with FoundationDB?\"\n```\n\nLaunch implementation worktrees:\n\n```bash\nmarvin implement --agents \"claude-code,codex\"\n```\n\nInside one of those worktrees:\n\n```bash\nmarvin work\n```\n\nIf you stay in the main repo after planning, `/marvin work` also works there. If multiple plan collabs exist for the repo, marvin prompts you to choose one.\n\n## Live overview\n\n`plan`, `plan-tui`, `investigate`, and `investigate-tui` all use the same live overview screen when running interactively:\n\n- the main document\n- the timeline\n- live transcripts\n- stance history\n\nUse the TUI when the user wants a form-based setup flow instead of flags.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `marvin implement` is setup-only. Use normal git workflows to compare, merge, and clean up implementation branches.\n- All commands output JSON by default unless the command is intentionally interactive.\n- Supported agent types are `claude-code`, `codex`, and `gemini-cli`.\n"}}
